  "final_message": "Your hunch is right. The dip is almost entirely three ports, and only in **Q2 2024**. Q2 2023 was normal.\n\n**The dip.** In 2024, Q2 had 9,622 TEU. The other three quarters of 2024 averaged about 12,770, so Q2 was down about 3,150 TEU (\u221225%). Shipment count and value fell by similar amounts.\n\n**Where it came from (TEU):**\n\n| Port | Avg of other 2024 quarters | Q2 2024 | Change |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| Brackwater | ~1,700 | 466 | \u221273% |\n| Fennick | ~1,750 | 366 | \u221279% |\n| Ivelholm | ~1,780 | 375 | \u221279% |\n| **Other 7 ports combined** | ~7,540 | 8,415 | **+12%** |\n\n- Brackwater, Fennick and Ivelholm lost about 4,000 TEU between them. That's more than the whole dip, because the other seven ports were slightly *above* normal in Q2.\n- **Timing:** the three ports went from 22 shipments in March to 5 a month in April, May and June, then back to 21 in July. That looks like one event lasting exactly a quarter, not a gradual slowdown.\n- **Carrier type:** container, bulk and tanker shipments all fell at those ports. That points to a problem at the ports themselves (closure, strike, congestion) rather than one cargo segment.\n\nThe overall drop from late 2023 into January 2024 is a separate, more spread-out softening, not part of this.\n\nI didn't change `notebooks/explore.py`. I ran these as one-off polars queries on `data/shipments.csv` outside the notebook. If you want, I can add cells with the port-by-quarter table and a monthly chart for the three ports.",
